### What is declarative transaction management?

Declarative transaction management means you annotate a method with `@Transactional` and let the AOP proxy start, commit, and roll back the transaction around it. You write only business logic; Spring handles the connection lifecycle. This is the standard and most common approach in Spring Boot applications.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(sender);
        accountRepo.save(receiver);
    }
}
```

The proxy calls `setAutoCommit(false)` before the method, calls `commit()` on normal return, and calls `rollback()` if a RuntimeException propagates out.

### What is programmatic transaction management?

Programmatic transaction management means you control the transaction boundary yourself using `TransactionTemplate` or `PlatformTransactionManager`. You wrap the transactional code inside a callback, and the template manages begin, commit, and rollback for that specific block.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ProgrammaticTransferService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public ProgrammaticTransferService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    public BigDecimal transferAndReturnBalance(
            String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return txTemplate.execute(status -> {
            Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
            Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
            sender.debit(amount);
            receiver.credit(amount);
            accountRepo.save(sender);
            accountRepo.save(receiver);
            return sender.getBalance();
        });
    }
}
```

The `TransactionTemplate` is thread-safe. Create it once in the constructor and reuse it for every call.

### How does TransactionTemplate compare to @Transactional?

Both achieve the same result: atomic database operations. The difference is where the transaction boundary is defined.

| Aspect | @Transactional (Declarative) | TransactionTemplate (Programmatic) |
|--------|-----------------------------|------------------------------------|
| Boundary | Entire method | Lambda block only |
| Mechanism | AOP proxy intercept | Direct template call |
| Self-invocation | Broken (proxy bypassed) | Works (no proxy involved) |
| Multiple TXs in one method | Not possible | Each execute() is independent |
| Readability | Clean, one annotation | More verbose, explicit |
| Configuration | Annotation attributes | Template setter methods |
| Rollback | Exception propagation | Exception or setRollbackOnly() |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ComparisonService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public ComparisonService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Declarative: entire method is one transaction
    @Transactional
    public void transferDeclarative(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
    }

    // Programmatic: only the lambda block is transactional
    public void transferProgrammatic(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.info("Starting transfer"); // outside the transaction
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
            Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
            sender.debit(amount);
            receiver.credit(amount);
        });
        log.info("Transfer committed"); // outside the transaction
    }
}
```

### What is execute vs executeWithoutResult?

The `execute` method takes a `TransactionCallback<T>` and returns a value of type T. Use it when the transactional block must produce a result.

The `executeWithoutResult` method takes a `Consumer<TransactionStatus>` and returns void. It was added in Spring 5.2 to avoid the pattern of `execute(status -> { ...; return null; })`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountQueryService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountQueryService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // execute: returns a value
    public BigDecimal getBalanceInTx(String accountId) {
        return txTemplate.execute(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId);
            return account.getBalance();
        });
    }

    // executeWithoutResult: void operation
    public void freezeAccount(String accountId) {
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId);
            account.setStatus("FROZEN");
            accountRepo.save(account);
        });
    }
}
```

### How does programmatic TX handle batch processing?

In batch processing, a single `@Transactional` around the entire batch means one failure rolls back everything. `TransactionTemplate` solves this by making each chunk an independent transaction. If chunk 3 fails, chunks 1 and 2 are already committed.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class InterestBatchService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 500;

    public InterestBatchService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    public void applyMonthlyInterest(BigDecimal rate) {
        List<Account> allAccounts = accountRepo.findAllSavings();
        int total = allAccounts.size();
        int committed = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < total; i += BATCH_SIZE) {
            int start = i;
            int end = Math.min(i + BATCH_SIZE, total);
            List<Account> chunk = allAccounts.subList(start, end);

            try {
                txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
                    for (Account acct : chunk) {
                        BigDecimal interest = acct.getBalance()
                            .multiply(rate);
                        acct.credit(interest);
                        accountRepo.save(acct);
                    }
                });
                committed += chunk.size();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                log.error("Batch {} failed, skipping", start / BATCH_SIZE, e);
                // Earlier batches are already committed
            }
        }
        log.info("Interest applied to {} of {} accounts", committed, total);
    }
}
```

```text
Interest applied to 49500 of 50000 accounts
```

### What are the rollback rules?

Both declarative and programmatic share the same default: RuntimeException and Error trigger rollback; checked exceptions trigger commit.

In declarative mode, override with `rollbackFor`:

```java
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void transferDeclarative(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount)
        throws TransferException {
    // checked TransferException now triggers rollback
}
```

In programmatic mode, override with `status.setRollbackOnly()`:

```java
txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
    Account sender = accountRepo.findById(fromId);
    if (sender.getBalance().compareTo(amount) < 0) {
        status.setRollbackOnly(); // force rollback without throwing
        return;
    }
    sender.debit(amount);
    accountRepo.save(sender);
});
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: using @Transactional when batch needs per-chunk commits

```java
// WRONG: one failure rolls back all 50,000 accounts
@Transactional
public void applyInterestBad(BigDecimal rate) {
    for (Account acct : accountRepo.findAllSavings()) {
        BigDecimal interest = acct.getBalance().multiply(rate);
        acct.credit(interest);
        accountRepo.save(acct);
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: use TransactionTemplate for per-chunk commits
public void applyInterestCorrect(BigDecimal rate) {
    List<Account> accounts = accountRepo.findAllSavings();
    for (int i = 0; i < accounts.size(); i += BATCH_SIZE) {
        List<Account> chunk = accounts.subList(i, Math.min(i + BATCH_SIZE, accounts.size()));
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            for (Account acct : chunk) {
                acct.credit(acct.getBalance().multiply(rate));
                accountRepo.save(acct);
            }
        });
    }
}
```

#### Mistake 2: mixing both approaches redundantly

```java
// WRONG: @Transactional already manages the TX, TransactionTemplate creates a nested one
@Transactional
public void doubleManaged() {
    txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
        // this starts a second transaction if propagation is REQUIRES_NEW
    });
}
```

Pick one approach per method. Do not combine declarative and programmatic on the same boundary.

### When should you use programmatic over declarative?

Use programmatic when:

- Batch processing requires per-chunk commits so earlier progress survives a later failure.
- Self-invocation: a method calls another method on the same bean, and you need the inner call to have its own transaction.
- Mixed logic: part of the method is transactional and part is not, and you need the boundary at a finer grain than the whole method.
- Dynamic rollback: you want to decide at runtime whether to roll back based on business rules without throwing an exception.

Use declarative for everything else. It is cleaner, less error-prone, and the standard approach.

```java
// Programmatic solves self-invocation
@Service
public class SettlementService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    public SettlementService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager, TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    // No proxy needed: each trade settles in its own TX
    public void settleAll() {
        List<Trade> pending = tradeRepo.findPending();
        for (Trade trade : pending) {
            txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
                trade.settle();
                tradeRepo.save(trade);
            });
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `TransactionTemplate` wraps `PlatformTransactionManager` and is the recommended programmatic API. Using `PlatformTransactionManager` directly with `getTransaction()`, `commit()`, and `rollback()` is rarely needed.
- The template is thread-safe once configured. Create it once per service in the constructor.
- Configure propagation, isolation, timeout, and readOnly on the template with setter methods.
- In reactive applications (WebFlux), use `TransactionalOperator` instead of `TransactionTemplate` because reactive code does not have ThreadLocal-bound connections.
- Prefer declarative for clarity and reserve programmatic for batch commits, self-invocation fixes, and dynamic boundaries where the annotation cannot express what you need.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.TransactionDefinition;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;

@Configuration
public class TxTemplateConfig {

    @Bean
    public TransactionTemplate batchTxTemplate(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
        TransactionTemplate template = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        template.setPropagationBehavior(
            TransactionDefinition.PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW);
        template.setTimeout(30);
        return template;
    }
}
```

